    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thrips makes sense. i looked under the leaves but didnt really see much, just some tiny dots. if it is bugs, are they usually active during the day or do they hide somewhere when people are around? its just weird because the greenhouse is kept pretty clean and we monitor it constantly, but these holes just keep appearing out of nowhere.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Thrips are tiny pests that often hide in the crevices of leaves, along the plant veins, or in dense foliage just coming in from nearby flowering plants rather than in the center of your greenhouse. They feed on plant sap by puncturing cells, which causes those speckled spots and holes you're seeing, and they are active at night so you might catch them more easily late in the day. Simple measures like spraying the undersides of leaves with strong water or applying a light oil spray can help dislodge these hard-to-see insects and stop the damage from spreading.",
